  Nothing in this repo produces an ID. The options differ in what you'd have to redo later:

  - [1mThe server issues it.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m eventually POSTs to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m and the response carries the real [38;5;153muserId[39m; the session
    module stores whatever came back. Correct long-term shape and the only one where the ID means the same thing across
    devices. Cost: until the endpoint exists, the stub has to fake a response — so we design against a response
    contract that isn't built yet.
  - [1mThe client generates it.[0m A [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m minted at login and kept in storage. Self-contained, works today,
    zero backend dependency. But it identifies a [3mbrowser[0m, not a person — the same user on a second device is a different
    ID, and swapping to server IDs later invalidates every stored value.
  - [1m[38;5;153musername[39m is the ID.[0m No new value at all; the session stores the username. Simplest, works today, and it's already
    what [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns. Costs: breaks if usernames are ever editable, and leaks a human-readable identifier into
    storage.

  Cheap to change later: the [3mstorage[0m mechanism and the module's API. Expensive to change later: the ID's meaning —
  anything already persisted under the old scheme is garbage after a switch, so this is the decision worth getting right
  now.

  [1mMy recommendation: design for the server-issued ID, with the stub faking that response shape.[0m It costs the same today
  as the other two, and it's the only one that doesn't force a migration when [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m becomes real. I'd have
  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m return [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m and the session module consume that — which, notably, still isn't a [38;5;153muserId[39m
  [3mparameter[0m on [38;5;153mlogin()[39m.
